      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can reduce this to\n```\nvoid foo() {\n    enum class E {kA};\n    auto l \u003d [](auto\u0026 self, E e \u003d E::kA) -\u003e void {};\n    l(l);\n}\n```\nwhich produces\n```\n\u003csource\u003e(3): error C2653: \u0027E\u0027: is not a class or namespace name\n\u003csource\u003e(3): note: the template instantiation context (the oldest one first) is\n\u003csource\u003e(4): note: see instantiation of default argument expression required for \u0027void foo::\u003clambda_1\u003e::operator ()\u003cfoo::\u003clambda_1\u003e\u003e(_T1 \u0026,foo::E) const\u0027\n        with\n        [\n            _T1\u003dfoo::\u003clambda_1\u003e\n        ]\n\u003csource\u003e(3): error C2065: \u0027kA\u0027: undeclared identifier\n```\nMoving the enum declaration out of the function, not passing `self`, or removing the default-ness of the parameter all alleviate the compiler issue. This appears to be consistent since v19.24 when `/Zc:lambda` was introduced. Other compilers seem fine with it.",
